DESCARTES, LA GEOMETRIE (1637)
Equations, notation, and fundamentals
516.3 Analytic geometry; historical works

FUNDAMENTALS
------------
Letters denote LENGTHS of line segments (positive quantities).
Known lengths:     a, b, c, d, ...
Unknown lengths:   x, y, z, ...
Powers:            aa or a^2  (square)
                   a^3, a^4, ...
Equality originally written with a special mark; now "=".
Dimensional break with the Greeks: a^2 and a^3 are treated as lengths,
not as areas or volumes.
A unit segment may be chosen so that products and quotients remain lengths.
Preferred form of an equation: all terms on one side, equal to 0.

ARITHMETIC AS GEOMETRY (Book I)
-------------------------------
Multiplication, division, and square root are constructed with similar
triangles (Thales) once a unit length is fixed.

CANONICAL QUADRATIC ("PLANE") EQUATIONS AND SOLUTIONS
-----------------------------------------------------
Type 1
  z^2 = a z + b^2
  z   = (1/2)a + sqrt( (1/4)aa + bb )

Type 2
  y^2 = -a y + b^2
  y   = -(1/2)a + sqrt( (1/4)aa + bb )

Type 3
  z^2 = a z - b^2
  z   = (1/2)a + sqrt( (1/4)aa - bb )
  z   = (1/2)a - sqrt( (1/4)aa - bb )

(Two positive roots when the discriminant is positive.)
Each solution is also given by a ruler-and-compass construction.

REDUCED FORMS HE LISTS FOR A SINGLE UNKNOWN
-------------------------------------------
z    = b
z^2  = -a z + b^2
z^3  = a z^2 + b^2 z - c^3
z^4  = a z^3 - c^3 z + d^4
...

GENERAL METHOD FOR A GEOMETRIC PROBLEM
--------------------------------------
1. Assume the construction already finished.
2. Name every needed segment (known and unknown).
3. Write relations until one unknown remains, equal to a known
   expression or to a power of that unknown.
Need as many independent equations as unknowns; otherwise the
problem is under-determined and free lengths may be chosen.

PAPPUS LOCUS  (origin of the pair x, y)
---------------------------------------
Given lines in the plane.
From a moving point C draw segments to those lines at prescribed angles.
Call two principal segments:
  AB = x     (along a chosen reference line)
  BC = y
Every other "distance" becomes a linear form
  a_i x + b_i y + c_i

Four-line condition (product of two distances proportional to product
of the other two):
  y (a1 x + b1 y + c1)
    = k (a2 x + b2 y + c2)(a3 x + b3 y + c3)

Expanded (quadratic in x):
  x^2 P0(y) + x P1(y) + P2(y) = 0

Concrete four-line example he obtains (hyperbola):
  y y = c y - (c/b) x y + a y - a c

Five or more lines raise the degree; the locus remains "geometric"
provided the equation is algebraic.

TANGENTS AND NORMALS  (circle method, Book II)
----------------------------------------------
Curve:     f(x, y) = 0
Point:     P = (x0, y0)
Normal meets the axis at Q = (v, 0)

Circle centred at Q through P:
  (x - v)^2 + y^2 = (x0 - v)^2 + y0^2

Eliminate one variable between the circle and the curve.
The resulting polynomial in the remaining variable must have a
DOUBLE ROOT at the point of contact (discriminant zero, or shared
root with the derivative). That condition determines v.
The tangent is the line through P perpendicular to PQ.

This is the first general algebraic method for tangents to
algebraic curves.

THEORY OF EQUATIONS  (Book III)
-------------------------------
Write every polynomial equation as
  P(z) = 0

NUMBER OF ROOTS
  An equation of degree n has n roots
  (real positive, real negative, and imaginary counted).
  Factorisation:
    (z - a)(z - b)...(z - s) = 0

DESCARTES' RULE OF SIGNS
  Number of positive real roots  <=  number of sign changes
      in the coefficient sequence of P(z)
      (difference is a non-negative even integer).
  Number of negative real roots  <=  number of sign changes
      in P(-z).

DEPRESSION OF A QUARTIC
  After removing the cubic term:
    z^4 + p z^2 + q z + r = 0
  Assumed factorisation:
    (z^2 + a z + b)(z^2 - a z + c) = 0
  Leads to a cubic resolvent in the unknown a^2.

TWO MEAN PROPORTIONALS between a and b
  a/x = x/y = y/b
  hence
    x^2 = a y
    y^2 = b x
    x y = a b
  Solved by intersecting a parabola with a circle (or hyperbola).

CLASSIFICATION OF CURVES (Book II)
----------------------------------
Geometric curves : those given by an algebraic equation in two
                   variables (constructible from lower-order curves).
Mechanical curves: transcendental (spiral, cycloid, ...).
Geometric curves are classified by the degree of their equation.
